¶
'''ballast''' (bal′ast), ''v. t.'' [Early mod. E. also ''balast'', and, with loss of ''t'', ''ballas'' (pret. and pp. ''ballased'', sometimes ''ballast'', ppr. ''ballasing''), ''ballasse'', ''ballace'', ''balase'', etc., = G. D. Flem. LG. ''ballasten'' = Dan. ''ballaste'', ''baglaste'' = Sw. ''barlasta''; from the noun.] '''1.''' To place ballast in or on; furnish with ballast: as, to ''ballast'' a ship; to ''ballast'' a balloon; to ''ballast'' the bed of a railroad. See the noun.
{{smaller block|The road was so perfectly ''ballasted'' with stone that we had no dust. {{right|''C. D. Warner'', Roundabout Journey, p. 3.}}}}
'''2.''' Figuratively: (''a'') To give steadiness to; keep steady.
{{smaller block|’Tis charity must ''ballast'' the heart.
{{right|''Hammond'', Sermons, p. 611.}}}}
(''b'') To serve as a counterpoise to; keep down by counteraction.
{{smaller block|<poem>Now you have given me virtue for my guide,
And with true honour ''ballasted'' my pride. ''Dryden.''</poem>}}
'''3†.''' To load; freight.—'''4.''' To load or weigh down.
{{smaller block|When his belly is well ''ballaced'', and his brain rigged a little, he sails away withal.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Ind. to Every Man in his Humour.}}}}
{{smaller block|These yellow rascals [coins] must serve to ''ballast'' my purse a little longer. {{right|''Scott'', Old Mortality, ix.}}}}

'''ballast†''' (bal′ast), ''pp.'' Ballasted.
{{smaller block|Who sent whole armadas of carracks to be ''ballast''.
{{right|''Shak.'', C. of E., iii. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Hulks of burden great,
Which Brandimart rebated from his coast,
And sent them home ''ballast'' with little wealth.</poem>
{{right|''Greene'', Orlando Furioso.}}}}

